    "reading": {
        "body": "Daily downloads across Hugging Face average roughly two billion, with small daily variance suggesting a stable baseline of model use rather than spiky demand. The 16,369 models hosted span embedding systems, small language models, and specialized architectures, dominated by a tiny set. Sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 alone accounts for 250 million downloads, twelve times more than the second-ranked model. This concentration reveals what practitioners actually deploy: small, efficient embeddings and sub-billion-parameter models vastly outweigh frontier LLMs in real usage.\n\nThe downloads do not track compute consumption. A single training run of a large model consumes more power than millions of inference calls on a small one. The data shows adoption breadth, not training or operational intensity. Embedding models and small language models dominate because they run on modest hardware and solve immediate problems. This tells you where the ecosystem's daily activity concentrates, not where its compute or power footprint concentrates.\n\nApache 2.0 and MIT licenses govern 7,080 and 2,303 models respectively, representing 56 percent of the catalog. Proprietary and restrictive licenses remain marginal. This reflects the open-source incentive structure of the community, but does not constrain commercial use or indicate who trains or funds the leading models. Licensing breadth shows community composition, not power consumption or infrastructure requirements.",
